{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773326891,"nonce":"M0GCrADdYC9EBTjExix+Ae+L6uo=","title":"Watcher process ignores SIGTERM/SIGINT when backgrounded","message":"## Problem\n\nWhen the watcher is run via `nightshift watcher \u0026` (backgrounded), it becomes unkillable:\n\n1. Ctrl+C doesn't reach it (backgrounded process is not in the foreground process group)\n2. `kill \u003cpid\u003e` sends SIGTERM, but Python's default handler raises `SystemExit` which can't interrupt a blocking `subprocess.run([\"git-bug\", ...])` call\n3. The git-bug lock retry loop (3 retries × 5s sleep) compounds the delay\n\n## Root Cause\n\n- `cmd_watcher` in `host/cli.py` launches the watcher via `subprocess.run()` with no signal forwarding\n- The watcher main loop (`host_watcher.py:89`) is `while True` with no SIGTERM/SIGINT handler\n- When stuck in `GitBugTracker._run()` → `subprocess.run([\"git-bug\", ...])`, SIGTERM can't interrupt until the subprocess finishes\n- No child process cleanup on exit\n\n## Fix\n\n- Add a SIGTERM/SIGINT handler in `host/watcher/main.py` that:\n  1. Sets a shutdown flag checked by the main loop\n  2. Kills any in-flight child subprocesses (git-bug, docker)\n  3. Exits cleanly\n- Use `subprocess.Popen` + poll pattern instead of blocking `subprocess.run` in `GitBugTracker._run()` so signals can be processed during git-bug calls\n- Consider using `os.setpgrp()` + `os.killpg()` for clean process group termination\n\n## Requirement\n\nREQ-002 (Session lifecycle management) — watcher is part of the session lifecycle","files":null}]}